"Speeding Up Sum-Check Proving (Extended Version) (https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/587)\nciteturn7view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/587\",\"lineno\":29}); Total lines: 82\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2026/587\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Speeding Up Sum-Check Proving (Extended Version)】\nL32: \nL33: Quang Dao, Carnegie Mellon University\nL34: \nL35: Zachary DeStefano, New York University\nL36: \nL37: Suyash Bagad, Ingonyama\nL38: \nL39: Yuval Domb, Ingonyama\nL40: \nL41: Justin Thaler, Georgetown University, a16z crypto research\nL42: ##### Abstract\nL43: The sum-check protocol is a foundational primitive in modern cryptographic proof systems, but its prover-side cost has emerged as a concrete bottleneck. This paper introduces three complementary techniques that significantly reduce sum-check proving time and memory, especially in the context of zero-knowledge virtual machines (zkVMs).\nL44: First, for applications involving products of many multilinear polynomials, we develop a new algorithm that significantly reduces the number of field multiplications required for proving. Second, we develop a \"small-value sum-check prover\" algorithm. This significantly speeds up the prover in the common setting where the polynomials being summed evaluate to 64 or 32-bit integers, or to elements of a small sub-field within a larger extension field.\nL45: Even outside of the small-value setting, this algorithm yields a faster \"streaming prover\", by which we mean a small-space algorithm that applies whenever the terms being summed can be enumerated in small space (as arises, for example, in zkVM applications). Third, we nearly eliminate prover overhead in the ubiquitous case where one factor is an equality polynomial by exploiting its decomposable tensor structure.\nL46: We implement these techniques in Jolt, a state-of-the-art zkVM, and evaluate their performance. In Jolt, we observe over an order of magnitude runtime speedup and memory reduction on the Spartan sub-protocol, and $1.7\\times$ to $2.2\\times$ speedups for a key high-degree sum-check sub-protocol in the Shout batch-evaluation argument.\nL47: Note: This manuscript directly combines and improves upon results from prior works: 2025/1117, 2024/1046, and 2024/1210\nL48: ##### Metadata\nL49: Available format(s)\nL50:     【17†PDF】\nL51: Category\nL52:     【18†Applications】\nL53: Publication info\nL54:     Preprint.\nL55: Keywords\nL56:     【19†sum-check】【20†snark】【21†zkvm】【22†delayed-reduction】【23†jolt】【24†spartan】【25†twist-shout】\nL57: Contact author(s)\nL58:     qvd @ andrew cmu edu\nL59: zd2131 @ nyu edu\nL60: suyash @ ingonyama com\nL61: yuval domb @ gmail com\nL62: justin r thaler @ gmail com\nL63: History\nL64:     2026-03-25: approved\nL65:     2026-03-24: received\nL66:     【26†See all versions】\nL67: Short URL\nL68:     【27†https://ia.cr/2026/587†ia.cr】\nL69: License\nL70: \nL71: 【28†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL72: BibTeX 【29†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL73: \nL74:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2026/587,\nL75:           author = {Quang Dao and Zachary DeStefano and Suyash Bagad and Yuval Domb and Justin Thaler},\nL76:           title = {Speeding Up Sum-Check Proving (Extended Version)},\nL77:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2026/587},\nL78:           year = {2026},\nL79:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/587}\nL80:     }\nL81: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.37710] zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37710)\nciteturn7view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37710\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 164\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Hardware Architecture\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 26 Oct 2026]\nL9: # Title:zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Samarth Narang】, 【6†Alhad Daftardar】, 【7†Brandon Reagen】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models, by Samarth Narang and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:SumCheck is a central prover primitive in zero-knowledge proofs, exposing table-parallel work that maps naturally to GPUs but becomes increasingly arithmetic-intensive as finite fields widen. We study how this shift affects the cost of programming SumCheck in Triton rather than CUDA.\nL17: Our benchmark, zkDuel, provides matched implementations of 25 zkPHIRE-derived constraints alongside diagnostic polynomials, covering 32-, 64-, 128-, and 256-bit prime fields, with independent CPU-reference validation and cross-backend checksum gating. Of 800 configurations attempted on an NVIDIA H100, 758 complete with matching checksums.\nL18: CUDA's median advantage over Triton falls from 3.5x at 32 bits to 1.4x at 256 bits, and Triton is faster on 19 large-table configurations; the same convergence appears on an A100, where the gap falls from 4.4x to 1.6x. Profiling shows that the small-field gap largely reflects host launch overhead and redundant modular additions in Triton's original block reduction, whose relative costs diminish as multi-limb Montgomery work grows.\nL19: Replacing that reduction with 【10†this http URL†tl.reduce】 brings Triton's median kernel time within 8% of CUDA on both GPUs, while specialized point interpolation improves 256-bit evaluation kernels by 1.55-1.75x. At cryptographic widths, these results show that a Python-embedded DSL can approach CUDA performance, although the largest fully unrolled constraints still expose compiler-scalability limits.\nL20: Comments:  | Accepted to HASP'26 at MICRO 2026\nL21: Subjects:  | Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【11†arXiv:2610.37710】 [cs.AR]\nL23:    | (or 【12†arXiv:2610.37710v1】 [cs.AR] for this version)\nL24:    | 【13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.37710†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | 【14†https://doi.org/10.1145/3857307.3857312†doi.org】 DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Samarth Narang [【15†view email】]\nL29: [v1] Mon, 26 Oct 2026 21:28:29 UTC (51 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models, by Samarth Narang and 2 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * 【8†View PDF】\nL38:   * 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL39:   * 【16†TeX Source】\nL40: \nL41: 【17†view license†creativecommons.org】\nL42: \nL43: ### Additional Features\nL44: \nL45:   * 【18†Audio Summary】\nL46: ### Current browse context:\nL47: \nL48: cs.AR\nL49: \nL50: 【19†< prev】   |   【20†next >】\nL51